{"context":"eka-cli ships the executable plugin contract (ADR-028, relocated to eka-core/plugin by ADR-030): plugins install into the plugin directory (~/.eka/plugins or $EKA_PLUGIN_DIR) via `eka plugin install`, verified against the release's SHA256SUMS.txt at install time, and the CLI talks to them through the machine-readable subcommands (`manifest --json`, `install \u003ckind\u003e --json`). What does not exist: plugin commands in the CLI surface. The eka-mcp plugin provides an MCP server (`eka-mcp serve`) and artifact installation, but the CLI has no way to expose plugin-provided commands in `eka --help` and dispatch to the plugin executable — a plugin's capabilities are invisible to the command line.\n\nplan:eka-mcp-production scope item 2 (B1) defines the mechanism: deferred registration via the manifest with a versioned dispatch protocol extension, the G1-G4 trust boundary (official-registry-only, hash at dispatch anti-TOCTOU, plugin dir over PATH, collision refusal, manifest cache TTL), and failure semantics that never degrade the CLI (\u003c=2s probe, fail-open skip, per-process memoize).\n\nplan:cli-experience is LOCKED (planning-state immutable — its container completed): the acceptance criterion's \"dynamic grouping amendment applied to plan:cli-experience\" cannot amend the locked plan. The dynamic GroupID amendment is therefore implemented as a runtime mapping in the CLI registration path (the Plugins group is added at registration time when plugin commands exist) instead of a plan content change — recorded as a decision below.","decision":"### Scope of this ADR\n\nThis ADR records the B1 command-registration mechanism, the G1-G4 trust boundary, the failure semantics, and the boundary against ADR-022/023 (command surface != behavior layer). Implementation lives in eka-cli cmd/plugin_registration.go (registration, dispatch, cache, memoize, checksum sidecar) and the install/update/remove finalize paths.\n\n### Decision 1 — B1 deferred registration\n\nPlugin commands are registered DEFERRED: at every root construction (per process, memoized), never at install time. For every installed official plugin, the CLI probes `manifest --json` (bounded: \u003c=2s deadline, the env whitelist, a 1 MiB output cap) and registers each declared command into the cobra tree under the dynamic \"Plugins\" group — the cobra GroupID mechanism from sto:help-ux (cmd/help_groups.go / renderCommandGroups). A plugin-free CLI renders exactly the five static intent groups; the Plugins group appears only when plugin commands exist. Registration never fails the CLI: every problem is a visible deterministic warning on stderr and a skip.\n\n### Decision 2 — Versioned dispatch protocol extension\n\nThe v1 manifest contract carries an ADDITIVE `commands` array (each: name, description, args) — the contract field stays \"v1\"; a plugin that does not emit it registers nothing. Dispatch contract: running a registered command executes `eka-\u003cname\u003e \u003cargs...\u003e [user args...]` under the bounded env whitelist (PATH, HOME, EKA_PLUGIN_DIR, SystemRoot — the CLI-side mirror of the eka-core runner's pluginEnv; never the CLI's secrets such as GH_TOKEN). The plugin's stdin/stdout/stderr inherit the CLI's streams, its exit code propagates, and the plugin owns its flags (the CLI never interprets them). The probe runs the plugin's manifest itself because the extension lives in the manifest JSON, which the eka-core ManifestContext parse does not expose raw; the core contract fields are validated the same way (contract version + name).\n\n### Decision 3 — G1: official-registry-only\n\nOnly plugins resolved through the built-in registry (plugin.OfficialRegistry) register commands. Third-party plugins never register — the registry is the trust decision (ADR-028), and command registration inherits it.\n\n### Decision 4 — G2: hash verified at dispatch (anti-TOCTOU)\n\nThe install/update finalize records the installed binary's SHA-256 in a sidecar (\u003cdir\u003e/.eka-\u003cname\u003e.sha256 — the leading dot keeps it invisible to the eka-* executable scans). Registration verifies the binary against the sidecar (a tampered binary never registers), and EVERY dispatch recomputes the binary's SHA-256 and compares it against the recorded checksum — a binary swapped between registration and dispatch refuses deterministically (exit 1). The expected checksum is memoized per process (bound into the dispatch command at registration); the hash itself is always recomputed fresh at the point of execution. A plugin without a recorded checksum (a legacy install predating B1) is skipped fail-closed with a visible warning and the reinstall hint.\n\n### Decision 5 — G3: plugin dir over PATH\n\nCommands register only from the plugin-directory instance (the install target). A plugin found only on PATH without a registry install is refused with a deterministic message and never registers. A PATH copy shadowing an installed plugin is ignored with a visible warning — the installed binary is the one that dispatches.\n\n### Decision 6 — G4: collision refusal + manifest cache\n\nA plugin command colliding with a built-in (or the cobra built-ins help/completion) or with another plugin's command refuses deterministically — first-wins in sorted order (plugins by name, commands by name), the loser is skipped with a visible warning naming both claimants. The manifest probe is cached per process with a TTL and invalidated on reinstall (the binary's file identity — size + mtime — changes), so repeated root constructions within one process never re-probe.\n\n### Decision 7 — Failure semantics (never degrade the CLI)\n\nThe registration probe is bounded by a \u003c=2s deadline (a hung plugin is killed, not waited on). A broken, hung, unverifiable or colliding plugin is SKIPPED with a visible deterministic warning on stderr — the CLI always continues and every other command works unchanged. Registration and dispatch decisions are memoized per process (the registration cache + the checksum bound into each dispatch command).\n\n### Decision 8 — Boundary vs ADR-022/023: command surface != behavior layer\n\nADR-022's AI Skill Pack is a BEHAVIOR layer: agent instructions (SKILL.md files) with zero CLI runtime surface — its commands (eka-discuss/eka-execute) are agent command FILES distributed by ADR-023's install commands, not CLI commands. B1 adds CLI command SURFACE for plugins: plugin-declared commands are executable surface that dispatches to the plugin binary. The two never overlap: B1 registers plugin-declared CLI commands; the skill pack remains instruction-only and is not registered by B1. The behavior layer (what an agent should do) stays distinct from the command surface (what the CLI can execute).\n\n### Decision 9 — Locked-plan decision: dynamic grouping as a runtime mapping\n\nplan:cli-experience is LOCKED (planning-state immutable, container completed) — the acceptance criterion's \"dynamic grouping amendment applied to plan:cli-experience\" cannot amend the locked plan. The dynamic GroupID amendment is implemented as a runtime mapping in the CLI registration path instead of a plan content change: the Plugins group is added to the root's group list at registration time when plugin commands exist (commandGroupsFor/hasPluginCommands in cmd/help_groups.go), and the root help and the landing share the same list so they never drift. The plan's static five-group model is unchanged; the dynamic group is a registration-time extension of it.","consequences":"- Plugin commands become first-class CLI surface: an installed official plugin's commands appear in `eka --help` and the landing under the Plugins group and dispatch to the plugin executable with a bounded environment.\n- The trust boundary is enforced at registration AND dispatch: official-registry-only (G1), checksum sidecar + fresh hash at every dispatch (G2), plugin dir over PATH (G3), collision refusal + TTL cache (G4). A tampered or swapped binary refuses deterministically; a broken plugin is skipped with a visible warning, never breaking the CLI.\n- The B1 extension is additive to the v1 manifest contract: existing plugins (including the current eka-mcp) emit no commands array and register nothing — backward compatible; the eka-mcp side (emitting the extension) is a follow-up under the same container.\n- The CLI-side probe mirrors the eka-core runner's env whitelist and output cap (the extension parse needs the raw manifest JSON, which the core parse does not expose) — a deliberate, documented mirror; the contract stays in eka-core.\n- Cost: every process start probes each installed official plugin once (\u003c=2s bound, memoized per process); a legacy plugin without a sidecar warns until reinstalled.\n- Risk: a plugin's self-reported command names/descriptions are attacker-controlled text — validated (safe charset) and terminal-sanitized before rendering.\n- Risk: the dynamic group is a runtime extension of the static group model — the shared renderer (renderCommandGroups) and the commandGroupsFor helper keep the root help and the landing in sync.","alternativesConsidered":"1. **Register at install time** (the plugin's commands are wired into the tree when `eka plugin install` runs): rejected — the tree is built per process; install-time registration would not survive the next invocation, and it would couple the install flow to the command surface.\n2. **A separate `commands --json` probe subcommand** (a new protocol verb instead of a manifest field): rejected — the manifest is the single self-description source (ADR-028); a second probe doubles the subprocess surface and splits the contract.\n3. **Trust PATH copies** (register from any discovered eka-* executable): rejected — a hand-copied binary bypasses the verified install path; G3 keeps the plugin dir authoritative.\n4. **No dispatch-time hash** (install-time verification only): rejected — the install-time gate cannot see a binary swapped after install; the sidecar + fresh hash at every dispatch closes the TOCTOU window.\n5. **Amend plan:cli-experience for the dynamic grouping**: rejected — the plan is LOCKED (immutable, container completed); the runtime mapping (Decision 9) delivers the same outcome without mutating immutable planning knowledge."}